That can be a powerful description of coercive systems, but I wouldn’t make it a universal law of people.

The interesting distinction is that size, money, institutional power, and violence are different currencies of leverage. Violence is the crudest because it can force immediate compliance. Money buys capability and coordination. Size creates inertia and bargaining weight. Institutional power makes decisions durable. But none of those necessarily produce respect—they often produce obedience, dependence, deterrence, or fear.

And there’s another currency that can beat all four in the right environment: legitimacy. If people voluntarily coordinate around you, you can acquire enormous effective power without having to threaten them. Information, reputation, competence, alliances, numbers, law, and economic leverage can compound into something much stronger than individual force.

So I’d sharpen your statement into:

Power gets attention.
Leverage gets negotiation.
Credibility gets listening.
Consequences get compliance.
Legitimacy gets voluntary cooperation.

For Pantheon terms, that suggests an important operator: don’t confuse the visible manifestation of power with the underlying ability to alter another actor’s decision landscape. Violence is only one—and usually very expensive—way of doing that.